86045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413691 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "faf9cf90-2f27-5a5a-8f4b-9a590b4a30eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B={2,4,6}$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $4$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |\n| $6$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "774de0b5-5c02-58cd-a5ae-6ac59d12337a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413692 #edb/ref 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2,4,6$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(2,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8df7bc4f-b191-56e9-905d-73750dc43c25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413693 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is defined only for $x>1.$ So, the set of first components contains all real numbers greater than $1$:\n\n$$\n{x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}\n$$\n\nHence, the domain is ${x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1}.$\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is continuous on $R,$ and has neither lower nor upper bounds. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the range is $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1071 "|  | $✠$ | $♣$ | $♠$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $✠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♣$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $♠$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined on the set ${✠,♣,♠},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(✠,♣)$\n- $(♣,♠)$\n- $(♠,✠)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93d6ac86-9246-53ae-8020-58de7dcc7070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413694 #edb/ref 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the sets $A={1,4,8}$ and $B={0,6,12},$ and the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x∣y}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $4$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |\n| $8$ | {{field-6}} | {{field-7}} | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9e513d0-e8df-5b01-bf4e-b611860d77a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413695 #edb/ref 1074 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}}$ and $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=0,6,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,0),$ $(1,6),$ and $(1,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=0,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,0)$ and $(4,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413696 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413696 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413696 #edb/ref 1071 "|  | $3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined over the sets ${2,4,8}$ and ${3,6},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(4,3)$\n- $(8,6)$\n- $(2,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759